'''Cagaarka Tower''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Daarta Cagaarka'', "Green Place Tower"), also known as '''Fotball Tower''' (Burawi: ''Daarta Fotbal'', "Football Tower"), is a 52-storey {{w|International Style (architecture)|International Style}} office building at 9915 [[Dhib Street]] in [[District 3, Samara|District 3]], [[Samara]], [[Burawa]]. Completed in 1964, it takes its name from the public park that previously occupied the site — the Cagaarka — which was demolished to make way for the tower. The building's two primary tenants are the [[Xiriirka Fotbal Burawi]] (XFB), which houses its headquarters and the [[Burawa national football team]] office in the building, and the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange]] (BFLE), which has been headquartered in the building since 1997.

== Location ==

Cagaarka Tower is located in [[District 3, Samara|District 3]], one of Samara's central administrative districts. District 3 borders [[Kankadadka]], the capital of [[Burawa]], and its proximity to the national capital has made it a natural location for regional government offices and institutional headquarters. The district houses a concentration of regional administrative offices, government agencies, and public-sector organisations that serve the broader Kankadadka-Samara metropolitan area.

The building's address at 9915 Dhib Street places it on one of District 3's principal commercial streets, within walking distance of the Samara Metro and the regional rail interchange.

== History ==

Cagaarka Tower was completed in 1964, during the period of post-[[Great Adonian War|war]] reconstruction and modernisation that reshaped Samara's central districts. The building was designed in the {{w|International Style (architecture)|International Style}}, characterised by its rectilinear form, reinforced concrete frame, ribbon windows, and absence of applied ornament — a style that dominated Burawi public architecture of the 1960s and reflected the socialist government's emphasis on functional, egalitarian design.

The building was originally constructed to house regional government offices for the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]]. Its 52 storeys made it one of the tallest buildings in Samara at the time of its completion, and its location in District 3 — adjacent to the regional capital — was chosen to facilitate coordination between Samara-based regional agencies and the Kankadadka government.

=== XFB and BFLE tenancy ===

The [[Xiriirka Fotbal Burawi]] (XFB) established its headquarters in Cagaarka Tower in 1989, relocating from smaller premises in central Samara. The move brought the federation's executive offices, national competitions staff, refereeing administration, licensing departments, and national-team offices into a single building. The tower's association with Burawi football became so strong that it acquired the informal nickname Fotball Tower.

The BFLE occupies floors 14 through 18, including the exchange's trading floor, compliance and regulatory offices, and administrative headquarters. The XFB occupies most of the lower and middle office floors, including the Burawa national football team offices on floors 11 through 13 and the federation's executive headquarters on floors 19 through 21. The shared building has facilitated close operational coordination between the two organisations, particularly during the annual draft period and the promotion/relegation season.

=== 2005 security incident ===

In 2005, Cagaarka Tower was the site of a security incident following a disputed regional competition match between [[FC Xudur]] and [[FC Doollo]]. A supporter from Doollo drove a rented truck through the front entrance of the building, destroying the entrance area and welcome desk and injuring two workers in the lobby; a pedestrian outside also sustained injuries. The driver was tackled and detained shortly after the impact. The incident led to tighter security screening at the tower and to the installation of additional vehicle barriers and access controls around major football administration buildings in Samara.

== Architecture ==

Cagaarka Tower is a reinforced concrete structure with a rectilinear plan and a curtain wall facade of dark-tinted glass and black precast concrete panels. The building's dark, glassy exterior distinguishes it sharply from the many {{w|Brutalist architecture|Brutalist}} buildings constructed in Samara during the same period, which favoured exposed raw concrete, rough textures, and massive geometric forms. Cagaarka Tower's International Style design — with its horizontal emphasis, flat roofline, minimal ornamentation, and reflective dark facade — presented a deliberate contrast: sleek and corporate rather than monumental and civic. The facade is divided into regular bays by vertical mullions, with ribbon windows on each floor providing uniform natural light to the office interiors.

The ground floor and mezzanine contain a public lobby, retail units, and the building's main entrance on Dhib Street. The BFLE's stadium kiosk — a physical share-trading station that operates on match days — is located in the lobby, providing walk-in trading access for supporters visiting the building.

